7. DEPTH ESTIMATION AND ENVIRONMENT UNDERSTANDING

Develop a browser-based depth estimation system.

The application should attempt to provide:

* Relative depth estimation.
* Near and far object classification.
* Visual depth maps.
* Object segmentation.
* Basic object detection.
* Temporal object tracking.
* Approximate spatial relationships.
* A point-cloud-like visualization when enough depth information exists.

Clearly distinguish between:

Relative depth

Estimate whether an object appears closer or farther away.

This may use image analysis, local contrast, perspective cues, and locally executed computer vision models.

Metric depth

Estimate an object’s distance in meters.

Only display metric measurements when a technically justified calibration or depth model supports them.

Never invent precise distances from ordinary RGB pixels.

3D reconstruction

Create an approximate 3D representation of the environment only when the available visual information supports it.

Do not pretend that a decorative point cloud is a real room reconstruction.

Object tracking

Track detected objects between successive camera frames and estimate their movement.

When reliable spatial tracking is impossible, clearly label the result as approximate.

